  "Question": "The number of values of \\(x\\) satisfying the equation\n\n\\[ \\frac{2x^2-10x}{x^2-5x}=x-3 \\]\n\nis:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "zero",
    "B": "one",
    "C": "two",
    "D": "three",
    "E": "an integer greater than \\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "First find where the original fraction is defined. Since\n\\[\nx^2-5x=x(x-5),\n\\]\nwe must have \\(x\\ne0,5\\). For every allowed value,\n\\[\n\\frac{2x^2-10x}{x^2-5x}\n=\\frac{2x(x-5)}{x(x-5)}=2.\n\\]\nThe equation therefore requires \\(2=x-3\\), giving \\(x=5\\). But this makes the original denominator zero, so it is not a solution. There are \\(\\boxed{0}\\) values of \\(x\\), choice \\(\\mathrm A\\).",
